## Replacing an instance method

Use exactly ONE `fix_at_line` call. Build `new_block` as: simplified function, then a blank line, then each helper definition — all sharing the original function's column-0 indent (the class-member indent, typically 4 spaces).

- `start_line` = first line of the original function (its `def` line, or the first decorator above it)
- `end_line`   = last line of the original function body
- `old_block`  = `""` (empty — trust the line range)
- `new_block`  = simplified function + blank line + helper(s), all at the same indent

Why one call, in-class: the EOF-append pattern places content at module level. For an instance method the simplified caller still does `self.<helper>(...)`, which only resolves to a class member. A module-level helper with `self` as first parameter is not a method — `self.<helper>(...)` will `AttributeError` at runtime. The post-edit syntax check rejects orphaned helpers; if you see "function(s) '<name>' have `self` as first parameter but are defined outside a class body" you used the wrong pattern.

## Appending a helper at EOF (free functions / `@staticmethod` only)

The simplest, safest pattern is "append after end of file":

- Run `file_editor view` to see the file. Note the highest line number it shows; call this `L_view`.
- Call `fix_at_line` with `start_line` = `end_line` = `L_view + 1`, `old_block` = `""`, `new_block` = `<helper source>`.
- The executor recognises this exact shape (start == end == total_lines + 1, empty `old_block`) as a deliberate "append after EOF" and appends without needing a content anchor.

Why `L_view + 1` and not `L_view`: `file_editor view` may display one trailing line beyond the file's actual content (a UI affordance for "you can append here"). Targeting `L_view + 1` lands cleanly past the file regardless of which convention the view uses.

After each append the file grows; re-`view` (or just add the helper's line count to your local `L_view`) before the next append.

Setting `start_line`/`end_line` to a line that contains existing function code overwrites that line. Don't do that. The append-at-EOF pattern is the safe default for module-level helpers.

If for some reason you must anchor on existing content (rare), use `start_line` = `end_line` = the line number of the LAST line that contains real code, set `old_block` = that line's exact text, set `new_block` = `<old_block>` + `\n\n` + `<helper source>`.

